tituents) is primarily defined by bonafide (natural) boundaries.  Examples: lobe of lung, osteon, acinus, submucosa, anterior leaflet of mitral valve, capsule of kidney, cortical bone, muscle fasciculus.5-17-02: OM- it may be necessary to add anatomical space as one of the requirements for classifying an organ part which consists of only one tissue type as an organ component. E.g. gastric gland, it consists of epithelium and lumen. Is gastric gland just a subdivision of epithelium?nOld definition as of 7.30.03:nOrgan part, which is countable and consist of a principal and one or more subsidiary tissues; (is divisible into basic tissues); connected to other organ components, it constitutes an organ subdivision.<br><b>non-English equivalent</b>: KB_AUTO-INSERT(3)_4646<br><b>:direct-template-slots</b>: receives attachment from")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:FMA_14065 "Cardinal organ part which is bounded predominantly by bonafide boundaries.  Examples: lobe of lung, osteon, acinus, submucosa, anterior leaflet of mitral valve, fibrous capsule of kidney, compact bone, striated muscle fasciculus.")
